WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.819
Today, we're going to be speaking with Chris
Smalls, who is a former Amazon employee who

00:00:04.819 --> 00:00:09.390
has become a leader of the e-commerce and
labor rights movement.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:14.730
Chris is going to tell us about his workforce
and his subsequent firing from Amazon.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:23.690
Now, of course, we haven't been able to independently
confirm Chris's story, but it is a compelling

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:24.690
one.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:29.869
And I am more than open to having someone
from Amazon on to talk to us about the things

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:31.960
that Chris is going to tell us.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:36.469
Chris, you have a background that started
similar to mine, which is your background

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:37.469
is in retail.

00:00:37.469 --> 00:00:42.930
I worked at the now defunct Circuit City when
I was much younger.

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.290
You worked at Wal-Mart and Target.

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:48.519
But let's talk a little bit about your work
for for Amazon.

00:00:48.519 --> 00:00:51.100
How did you come to work at Amazon?

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.049
I started working in Amazon back in twenty
fifteen.

00:00:54.049 --> 00:00:55.979
I came in entry level.

00:00:55.979 --> 00:00:58.629
I came from before Amazon.

00:00:58.629 --> 00:01:04.180
I was working at a grocery distribution warehouse
overnight graveyard shift that was unionized.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:09.549
But the union contract just wasn't what I
expected it to be.

00:01:09.549 --> 00:01:11.860
So I thought I have a better opportunity.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:12.990
At Amazon.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:17.369
I opened up the first building in Carterton,
Jersey, EWR nine.

00:01:17.369 --> 00:01:22.539
I stayed there for about two years and then
I transferred to Middle to Connecticut.

00:01:22.539 --> 00:01:24.450
And then my last facility was JFK, Staten
Island.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.119
So I've been with the company for almost four
and a half years.

00:01:28.119 --> 00:01:29.830
And what was your day to day like?

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:33.060
What kind of roles were you in?

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:39.620
So when I became when I first got hired, I
was entry level, I was what they call a picker,

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:47.130
I my job was to pick custom items in a timely
manner within the hour, hourly rate of about

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:50.969
then it was about 250 items an hour.

00:01:50.969 --> 00:01:53.710
Now is a lot more is about 400 now.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:55.890
But I did that for about six or seven months.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:02:01.710
And then I was promoted up to a process assistant
that I'm known as an assistant manager.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:05.380
And I was in that position for the last four
years now as an assistant manager.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.610
Were you supervising pickers?

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:14.750
Yes, I actually supervise in pickers and then
and expanded to a different operation, which

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:18.540
now I was in control of three different departments.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:24.640
So picking counting and also Florelle.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.820
So Floor Help was also a part of the department
as well.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:33.610
OK, so many people in my audience have probably
seen the articles that talk about the conditions

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:39.140
for the pickers and they include things like
you're not allowed to go to the bathroom and

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:44.040
there's this constant pressure to pick more
and more and more and the conditions are bad

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.560
and your breaks are monitored down to the
minute.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.930
Talk a little bit about what what of that
was true when you were there?

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:51.720
Is any of it not true?

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.300
No, it's 100 percent true.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.260
Amazon is completely off of metrics and numbers.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:01.540
They calculate everything it is is actually
calculated down to the second you know, the

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:07.750
second you stop scanning the item, your time
accumulates time.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:09.960
ALTEZ So I was a part of that machine.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:15.090
I very much had to monitor my associates in
my department.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:19.790
But like I said, I was just a supervisor that
related to them.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:23.420
So they respected the fact that I wasn't that
type of supervisor.

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:28.060
That will be hard on them if they came back
from break or whatever the case may be, because

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:32.810
I know how massive these buildings are and
how the working conditions can take a toll

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:34.060
on your body.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:40.550
Now, were you pressured even though you weren't
pressuring the pickers may be the way that

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:41.630
you would be expected to.

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:44.000
Did that cause a problem for you?

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:50.820
Absolutely, I mean, for the most part, it
caused a problem with my own personal career

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:56.130
growth because obviously management knew that
I would never side with them when it came

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:57.560
to certain things like that.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:02.720
And I was kind of like the black sheep out
of the supervisors.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:07.100
Everybody knew that I had a backbone and I
always spoke up for what was right.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:10.630
And I always sided with the employees at entry
level because that's where I started from.

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:17.150
I never forgot where I came from and it didn't
bother me, but it definitely hindered my career

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:18.150
there.

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:22.320
You know, I applied for a salaried manager
position there forty nine times and I was

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.790
denied every last one of them.

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:30.070
And it's probably because the fact that they
knew that I would never be fully pro Amazon

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:34.150
were what reason were you given the forty
nine times why you weren't going to be given

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:36.020
the salaried manager.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:43.630
Well, Wolf, for my own personal experiences,
it was it was basically bogus, a lot of the

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:47.740
interviews were set up for me to fail from
the beginning.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:52.740
A lot of them the interviews, they already
knew who they wanted in those positions before

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:53.790
I even applied.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:56.130
So it was a combination of a lot of different
things.

00:04:56.130 --> 00:05:03.300
Also, there was, you know, letters and management
above me that didn't like me as a person,

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.340
you know, whatever the case may be.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:10.140
So it was a lot of different things of why
I didn't get those positions.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:15.320
And I believe it's systemic, you know, systemic
racism that exists inside and outside as well.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:20.880
Over a million and a half employees worldwide
and only and then twenty six years of existence.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:23.660
Only eight percent of the management is black
or brown.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:28.230
So that just tells you right there, the higher
up the food chain, the less likely, you know,

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:29.360
you are to be colored.

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:35.490
So I even believe this for the first time
this past year, they have one black woman

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:37.480
that's on the senior team.

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:42.030
So that just tells you right there the chances
of me getting promoted up the ladder with

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:43.050
Slim and Chris.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.450
I don't I'm not I'm not familiar with the
numbers you're citing.

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:49.610
So I'm curious, when you say eight percent,
is that globally or in the United States?

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:50.610
That's globally.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:51.610
That's globally.

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:56.669
OK, do you know what it is in the United States?

00:05:56.669 --> 00:05:58.790
I would guess it would be a lot less than
that.

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:02.960
You know, I'm pretty sure it's not too many
of us at the top.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:08.310
You know, once again, in this this company's
twenty six or twenty seven years of existence

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:14.670
for the first time ever, they put one black
woman on the senior team, the team, they call

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:15.670
it.

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:17.430
So that just tells you right there they're
way behind the eight ball.

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:23.040
When it comes to diversity, when it comes
to management, were there ways in which working

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:30.169
at Amazon was better than when you did actual
customer facing retail like Wal-Mart and Target?

00:06:30.169 --> 00:06:35.330
Of course, you know, I worked at competitors
and I worked during the holiday season.

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:39.580
And, you know, nothing's worse than working
Black Friday, right.

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:47.230
At a time when you got customers trying to
get their items in the lines of that, everything's

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:48.430
chaos, chaos.

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:50.620
So we're going to warehouse.

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:52.630
You have to deal with customers.

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:53.630
It's a benefit.

00:06:53.630 --> 00:07:01.110
But once again, it's also calisthenics, you
know, so I used to tell my employees new hires,

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:04.360
you have a gym membership, you might want
to cancel it.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:08.510
Now, ultimately, you were fired by Amazon.

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:10.090
Can you talk about why?

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:16.880
Yeah, absolutely, you know, last year, the
beginning of a pandemic, late February, early

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:22.370
March, when this virus came into the country
in Seattle, killing the senior citizens over

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:23.370
there.

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:27.270
I was afraid over here on the East Coast and
I tried to raise health and safety concerns

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:30.290
going through the proper channels.

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:37.430
And after doing that for a few weeks, I tried
organizing workers inside the warehouse, sitting

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:42.550
in the cafeteria 10 hours a day off the clock,
telling many, as many workers as I can.

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:48.610
And I learned in a meeting with my bosses
that we had cases in the facility and they

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:54.370
didn't want me to tell anybody not to cause
a panic or disrupt business.

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:56.570
So I felt that I had to take further action.

00:07:56.570 --> 00:08:01.430
And after a whole week of advocating and pleading,
going back and forth with the general manager

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:07.030
of the building, they decided to quarantine
just me and everybody else.

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:11.710
And that didn't make sense to me, not even
the person I tried to work with, none of the

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:15.360
people in my department that was around the
person who was infected.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:16.500
It was just me.

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:17.550
So I do.

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:19.420
They were using me as a target.

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:24.940
And as a result, I held a protest on March
30 outside of my facility.

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:29.229
And then two hours later, I was terminated
over the phone.

00:08:29.229 --> 00:08:34.829
A week after that, Jeff Bezos and his top
general counsel, David Sapolsky, they tried

00:08:34.829 --> 00:08:39.860
to run a smear campaign on me, calling me
not smart, articulate, and make me the whole

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:42.569
face of the whole unionization efforts.

00:08:42.569 --> 00:08:44.360
So there's a couple of different things here,
right?

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:49.949
I mean, one is that the part about infections
at the facility and the other is trying to

00:08:49.949 --> 00:08:53.430
organize the the the workers.

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:55.209
So let's talk about the first one first.

00:08:55.209 --> 00:09:02.310
So you were told by managers not to talk about
the fact that there were infected employees?

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:03.490
Absolutely, yes.

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:09.440
On March 24, we had our Daily Sync meeting,
which we have every single day.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:14.019
And that's pretty much given us the rundown
for the rest of the day and how we're supposed

00:09:14.019 --> 00:09:15.019
to go.

00:09:15.019 --> 00:09:16.589
But on that day, it was different.

00:09:16.589 --> 00:09:22.959
That day they told us we had a case that was
actually two weeks prior for the first time,

00:09:22.959 --> 00:09:27.990
and they pretty much told the supervisors
not to say anything.

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:30.250
And I just felt that was wrong.

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:33.120
I felt like I had to say something that took
my stance there.

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:34.829
That was the last day I work for Amazon.

00:09:34.829 --> 00:09:36.490
So that was a supervisors meeting.

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:41.259
And you guys were told not to mention the
case to the employees you supervise?

00:09:41.259 --> 00:09:42.259
Absolutely.

00:09:42.259 --> 00:09:50.490
OK, and then separately, but around the same
time, you were working to organize the workers

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:51.640
there.

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:56.199
And what was the point of the organization
to approach management about virus related

00:09:56.199 --> 00:09:59.899
matters or to collectively bargain on salary
and benefits?

00:09:59.899 --> 00:10:03.390
Well, actually, that was the first time I
learned about it.

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:05.290
I haven't even started organizing yet.

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:07.300
That was March twenty four.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:11.929
I what I was doing was behind the scenes,
I was sending out emails to the governor,

00:10:11.929 --> 00:10:19.399
to Governor Cuomo office, to the CDC department
in our media outside and fight behind the

00:10:19.399 --> 00:10:20.399
scenes.

00:10:20.399 --> 00:10:24.670
It wasn't until after that meeting the next
day when I came back to the facility, I told

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:28.319
my supervisor I won't be working for the remainder
of the week.

00:10:28.319 --> 00:10:33.439
And that's when I started to go in the cafeteria
and tell all the workers that I came in contact

00:10:33.439 --> 00:10:39.089
with the truth that you we've been working
around somebody who tested positive from that

00:10:39.089 --> 00:10:40.089
point forward.

00:10:40.089 --> 00:10:45.860
What we did every morning, a small group of
us marched into the general manager's office

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:47.209
and voiced concerns.

00:10:47.209 --> 00:10:51.750
Yeah, I has this thing called Voice of Associates.

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:55.769
We associate allowed to write on the board
their complaints.

00:10:55.769 --> 00:10:56.850
So I said, you know what?

00:10:56.850 --> 00:11:02.179
Here is your your voiceover associates board
right here in person.

00:11:02.179 --> 00:11:06.100
Here's our complaint to be one of the building,
to just be simply closed down and sanitized,

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:07.600
we would have came back to work.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:08.600
No problem.

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:10.240
And that's all we wanted in the beginning.

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:12.070
But obviously a lot more.

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:18.249
Now, what was officially given to you is the
reason why you were being let go.

00:11:18.249 --> 00:11:23.939
They claim that I violated socially justice
policies that none of us ever read are ever

00:11:23.939 --> 00:11:30.339
implemented as supervisor, I'm supposed to
receive the policy that relay that information

00:11:30.339 --> 00:11:32.050
that I never did.

00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:35.839
So they didn't exist because they didn't know
what we were dealing with know.

00:11:35.839 --> 00:11:40.790
And that's all it was OK to understand that
they didn't know what they were dealing with,

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:46.459
how they handle it, but to ignore the fact
that workers were on the line to help allegations,

00:11:46.459 --> 00:11:52.000
you know, mothers that were pregnant, parents
that had children at home, including myself,

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:55.019
were afraid to come to work and refuse to
listen to us.

00:11:55.019 --> 00:11:57.309
That's a whole nother story.

00:11:57.309 --> 00:12:02.689
When it comes to what you're fighting for
now, if I understand correctly, you're looking

00:12:02.689 --> 00:12:08.110
to organize sort of a nonunion nationwide
group of workers.

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:12.059
Am I right that you're going the nonunion
approach and what made you decide to go about

00:12:12.059 --> 00:12:13.240
it that way?

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:21.029
Mean, you know, I feel like with Amazon is
such a large entity, a large business, that

00:12:21.029 --> 00:12:27.230
we actually would be kind of difficult for
one union to try to unionize the entire nation.

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:29.959
We're talking a hundred thousand workers.

00:12:29.959 --> 00:12:36.399
So I felt like an organization that has a
worker ribbon committee would be more beneficial

00:12:36.399 --> 00:12:42.339
for Amazon workers, something that's working
in employee controlled but union like structure.

00:12:42.339 --> 00:12:45.329
You know, I feel like that's the best way
possible.

00:12:45.329 --> 00:12:46.819
It's sort of like cutting out the middleman.

00:12:46.819 --> 00:12:50.930
No, no union dues or whatever the case may
be.

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:55.769
But workers are allowed to sit in on negotiation,
negotiate their own contracts.

00:12:55.769 --> 00:13:00.170
And then now we we also have enough power
workers power.

00:13:00.170 --> 00:13:05.040
The Congress say you're a social worker and
you have to go to work in the middle of a

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:06.040
pandemic.

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:07.040
Right.

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:10.139
We're going to say, no, we're not going to
work until we have, you know, PPE provided

00:13:10.139 --> 00:13:17.399
by the company, hazard pay, medical leave,
all these things that we're demanding before

00:13:17.399 --> 00:13:20.110
we consider ourselves essential workers.

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:21.399
So that's interesting.

00:13:21.399 --> 00:13:26.889
So absent that, actually, the actual union
with the dues, sort of like at what level

00:13:26.889 --> 00:13:28.220
would you see this working?

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:30.129
Would it be at the individual facility level?

00:13:30.129 --> 00:13:33.050
Would be at a regional level or how do you
how would you structure that?

00:13:33.050 --> 00:13:39.129
Yeah, you know, I thought about it, and I
think regional would be better if you had

00:13:39.129 --> 00:13:42.959
just one to break down the country into regions.

00:13:42.959 --> 00:13:48.189
You have a committee, a workplace committee
that represents that region because we know

00:13:48.189 --> 00:13:53.620
the cost of living in different parts of the
country is different of these committees.

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:56.279
Negotiate the contract and there you go.

00:13:56.279 --> 00:14:01.519
That be able to, you know, take care of the
mass of the employees that work for Amazon.

00:14:01.519 --> 00:14:02.610
What unionizing?

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:06.259
You have to do a facility by facility, and
that's going to be a longer process.

00:14:06.259 --> 00:14:09.519
As you can see what they're doing in Alabama,
they prolonging it.

00:14:09.519 --> 00:14:10.519
The union busting.

00:14:10.519 --> 00:14:12.259
Yeah, they're firing workers.

00:14:12.259 --> 00:14:13.639
They're hiring workers.

00:14:13.639 --> 00:14:16.279
They're doing all these things to disrupt
it.

00:14:16.279 --> 00:14:20.209
But if you have a workplace committee, that's
something that's built internally, it's going

00:14:20.209 --> 00:14:23.370
to be hard to bust that up.

00:14:23.370 --> 00:14:30.429
Did you ever see or experience or become aware
of sort of like not union busting, but maybe

00:14:30.429 --> 00:14:33.320
union prevention activity by Amazon?

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:41.089
Absolutely, but as a supervisor, you know,
I watched those training videos, we had to

00:14:41.089 --> 00:14:47.189
take tests, assessment tests, they try to
mix in certain questions that are obviously

00:14:47.189 --> 00:14:49.129
related to union busting.

00:14:49.129 --> 00:14:50.300
But I never applied them.

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:57.179
You know, like I said, I was I'm always on
the employee entry level position side, so

00:14:57.179 --> 00:14:58.869
I never applied these things.

00:14:58.869 --> 00:15:04.449
You know, obviously, there was management
that you applied that and they still do.

00:15:04.449 --> 00:15:06.910
But, you know, you have to understand what
you're dealing with.

00:15:06.910 --> 00:15:11.970
You're dealing with a company that does union
from the beginning.

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:16.259
And now we see that is very much necessary,
that they are unionized.

00:15:16.259 --> 00:15:21.220
Yeah, I remember in my early days at Circuit
City, they sat me down and mixed in with training

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:26.230
videos about like how to use the sale systems
and what the process is for requesting time

00:15:26.230 --> 00:15:27.230
off.

00:15:27.230 --> 00:15:31.689
They throw in videos about, you know, we want
to maintain a direct relationship with employees.

00:15:31.689 --> 00:15:34.359
We don't want any intermediaries or middlemen.

00:15:34.359 --> 00:15:37.800
And it was very it was very sophisticated,
in fact, the way that it was done.

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:38.800
Oh, yeah.

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:39.930
Yeah, the same thing.

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:45.259
You know, they mix in the questions like safety
and hazmat and all this training.

00:15:45.259 --> 00:15:49.749
I'm like, well, you know, you read between
the lines, right?

00:15:49.749 --> 00:15:50.749
Yeah.

00:15:50.749 --> 00:15:55.639
We've been speaking with Chris Smalls, former
Amazon employee, now a leader of the E Commerce

00:15:55.639 --> 00:15:56.639
labor rights movement.

00:15:56.639 --> 00:15:58.100
Chris, I really appreciate your time.

